    "main_topic": [
      {
        "h_title": "Welcome to Chiang Khan",
        "main_title": "Chiang Khan",
        "author": null,
        "language": "en",
        "level": 2,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Chiang Khan's historic temples, heritage houses, trendy cafes and Thai massage shops make it fun for a day of strolling or cycling.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "In the late afternoon enjoy the lengthy walkway along the Mekong and watch fishers casting out their nets before puttering home on longtail boats as the sun sinks behind the fluffy green hills. It is enchanting.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Near Chiang Khan is a mountain, Phu Tok, from which you have views of the Mekong - and there's no need to hike thanks to the songthaews that truck travellers to the viewpoint. A glass-floor skywalk set over the confluence of the Mekong and Hueang rivers is a thrill.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
      {
        "h_title": "Get off the beaten path in Loei",
        "main_title": "Chiang Khan",
        "author": null,
        "language": "en",
        "level": 5,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Loei province in Thailand's northeast draws a fraction of the visitors of the more popular destinations to the south. This makes it a thrilling place to visit with older (and more adventurous) kids.\nChiang Khan's historic temples, heritage houses, trendy cafes and Thai massage shops make it fun for a day of strolling or cycling. In the late afternoon enjoy the lengthy walkway along the Mekong and watch fishers casting out their nets before puttering home on longtail boats as the sun sinks behind the fluffy green hills. It is enchanting.\nNear Chiang Khan is a mountain, Phu Tok, from which you have views of the Mekong - and there's no need to hike thanks to the songthaews that can truck you to the viewpoint. A glass-floor skywalk set over the confluence of the Mekong and Hueang rivers is a thrill.\nFurther west, Phu Ruea National Park has waterfalls accessible via a road that winds up to a 1,365-metre summit with tremendous views. In the south of the province, climb to the 1,316-metre plateau at Phu Kradueng if you’re fit enough. There’s a campground there where temperatures can drop close to freezing at night. Alternatively, head to Suan Pha Hin Ngam for a tractor ride between vertical karst cliffs.\nBuses access some parts of Loei province, but renting a car in one of the larger provinces nearby, such as Khon Kaen or Udon Thani, would make it easier to explore the countryside. Loei also fits nicely into a wider tour of the Northeast that might include Nong Khai, Bueng Kan, Nakhon Phanom and other provinces along the Mekong.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      }
    ],
